---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import SiteScripts from '../components/SiteScripts.astro';
import { GITHUB_URL } from '../data/site';
import { LOGO_PATH } from '../data/brand';

const CONTACT_EMAIL = 'feedback@plozz.app';
const SENTRY_PRIVACY_URL = 'https://sentry.io/privacy/';
const GITHUB_PRIVACY_URL = 'https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement';
const LAST_UPDATED = 'September 23, 2026';
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy Policy — Plozz"
  description="How Plozz handles server logins, iCloud sync, approved device setup, connected services and optional crash reports. No Plozz account or usage analytics."
>
  <main class="legal">
    <div class="container">
      <a class="legal-home" href="/">
        <img src={LOGO_PATH} alt="" width="28" height="28" />
        <span>Plozz</span>
      </a>

      <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
      <p class="legal-updated">Last updated: {LAST_UPDATED}</p>

      <p>
        Plozz is a media player for Apple TV, iPhone and iPad. It connects to
        Jellyfin, Plex, Emby, Silo and network shares that you choose.
      </p>

      <h2>What we collect</h2>
      <p>
        Plozz has no usage analytics and no Plozz account. Crash reporting is
        optional and off by default. Playing media, fetching metadata,
        syncing settings and using connected services still require network
        requests. Those connections are described below; “no analytics”
        doesn’t mean the app never communicates with another service.
      </p>

      <h2>Crash reports &amp; diagnostics (optional, off by default)</h2>
      <p>
        Plozz includes an optional crash-reporting feature that is{' '}
        <strong>turned off by default</strong>. If you enable it under
        Settings → Help &amp; Diagnostics → Share Crash Reports and the app later
        crashes, freezes, or is shut down by the system, Plozz sends an
        anonymous diagnostic report so the problem can be found and fixed.
      </p>
      <p>
        These reports contain only the technical details of the failure plus
        your app version, operating-system version, and device model (for example
        “Apple TV 4K”). They never include your name, your server addresses,
        your logins or tokens, or anything about what you were watching.
      </p>
      <p>
        While crash reporting is turned on, you can also tap{' '}
        <strong>“Send to Developer”</strong> on the Recent Activity screen to
        send us a copy of the app’s recent activity log on demand — for example
        when we ask what Plozz was doing around a bug. This is the same list of
        log lines shown to you on that screen, with tokens, passwords, and
        server addresses removed, and nothing is sent unless you press the
        button. If crash reporting is off, the button is unavailable and no log
        can be sent.
      </p>
      <p>
        Crash reports and diagnostics are processed and stored by our
        error-monitoring provider,{' '}
        <a href={SENTRY_PRIVACY_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sentry</a>{' '}
        (Functional Software, Inc.), on servers in the United States, under
        Sentry’s privacy policy. You can turn crash reporting off at any time,
        which disables both the automatic reports and the “Send to Developer”
        button.
      </p>

      <h2>Bug reports &amp; feedback</h2>
      <p>
        When you choose to report a problem from inside Plozz, the app shows a
        link and QR code that open a pre-filled issue on our public{' '}
        <a href={`${GITHUB_URL}/issues`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>{' '}
        repository — only you decide whether to submit it. The pre-filled text
        includes a small, non-secret summary of your setup (app version,
        operating-system version, device model, and connected provider types) and a
        short excerpt — the last few lines — of the app’s recent activity log,
        with tokens, passwords, and server addresses removed. It never includes
        your full server addresses, logins, or tokens. Anything you post to
        GitHub is public and handled under{' '}
        <a href={GITHUB_PRIVACY_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub’s privacy policy</a>.
        You can also email us at{' '}
        <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>; we only receive
        what you choose to send.
      </p>

      <h2>Your server details</h2>
      <p>
        Login credentials are stored in the system Keychain on your device.
        They authorize connections to the services you choose and are not sent
        to the developer. Server users and profiles retain their server-owned
        access restrictions and watch history; local Plozz profiles don’t
        replace those identities.
      </p>

      <h2>iCloud and setting up another device</h2>
      <p>
        iCloud sync carries non-secret profiles, settings and server descriptors,
        not server login credentials. This data is handled through your Apple
        account. Watchlist sync and optional history connections are separate
        from whether a server login is stored in iCloud.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you approve device-to-device setup, eligible providers’ login
        credentials can transfer through that flow. Silo is an exception:
        its rotating login is never cloned. Only the server descriptor transfers,
        and the receiving device must pair with Silo independently. Pending Silo
        setup remains available in Settings → iCloud Sync.
      </p>

      <h2>Network connections</h2>
      <p>
        Plozz connects to your configured media servers and network shares
        for libraries, playback, subtitles and downloads. Providers receive
        the authentication and playback information needed to supply those
        features. Data handling on those servers is governed by their
        operators’ policies.
      </p>
      <p>
        Artwork, metadata, ratings and subtitle lookups can contact the
        services configured for those features. Connected trackers such as
        Trakt, AniList, MyAnimeList, Simkl and Last.fm receive the supported
        watchlist or listening/watching activity you enable. Seerr receives
        requests using the linked server user. Their own privacy policies apply.
      </p>
      <p>
        The website hosts the authorization relay used to connect AniList and
        MyAnimeList. That flow processes the authorization data needed to finish
        sign-in; it is separate from app usage analytics. The website itself is
        hosted by Cloudflare and requests the public GitHub star count from GitHub.
      </p>

      <h2>Children</h2>
      <p>
        Plozz is not directed at children and does not knowingly collect data
        from anyone.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>We may update this policy; changes will be posted at this URL.</p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />
  <SiteScripts />
</Layout>

<style>
  .legal {
    padding: 64px 0 88px;
  }

  .legal .container {
    max-width: 760px;
  }

  .legal-home {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 10px;
    font-weight: var(--w-strong);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    margin-bottom: 48px;
    transition: color 0.15s ease;
  }

  .legal-home:hover {
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .legal-home img {
    width: 28px;
    height: 28px;
    image-rendering: pixelated;
  }

  .legal h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 2.8rem);
    margin-bottom: 8px;
  }

  .legal-updated {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--text-dim);
    margin-bottom: 36px;
  }

  .legal h2 {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    margin-top: 36px;
    margin-bottom: 10px;
  }

  .legal p {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    margin-bottom: 14px;
  }

  .legal a {
    color: var(--brand);
  }

  .legal a:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }
</style>
